<h2>The statutory bridges: Sections 26 and 36</h2>
<p>Two short provisions make the Protection of Women from Domestic Violence Act, 2005 a connected statute rather than a sealed one. Section 36 provides that the Act is in addition to, and not in derogation of, any other law for the time being in force; Section 26 permits the reliefs the Act creates to be sought in any legal proceeding before a civil court, family court or criminal court. The mechanics of claiming reliefs in other forums are a subject in themselves. What matters here is the design signal the two sections send: Parliament expected a DV application, a divorce petition, a custody proceeding and maintenance claims to run side by side, with no forced election between them. Once proceedings coexist, their records inevitably travel into one another — and that traffic is where strategy lives.</p>

<h2>What a DV order actually decides</h2>
<p>The weight a DV order carries elsewhere depends on how it was made. An interim order under Section 23 — including an ex parte order — rests on a prima facie view of the application, affidavits and the domestic incident report, usually before any evidence is tested. A final order under Sections 18 to 22 follows the procedure of Section 28, with evidence recorded and cross-examination completed. The distinction travels with the order. A litigant who waves an interim residence order in the divorce court as though it were a finding of violence overstates it; a litigant who dismisses a post-evidence final order as a mere interlocutory indulgence understates it. Precision about what stage the DV court had reached, and what material it acted on, is the first discipline of cross-pollination.</p>

<h2>Into the divorce petition</h2>
<p>Cruelty as a ground under the Hindu Marriage Act, 1955 must be pleaded and proved in the divorce proceeding itself; no finding elsewhere relieves the petitioner of that burden. But the DV record supplies the raw material in at least three ways. First, the pleadings: statements made in the Section 12 application and in the reply are admissions of their makers, available under the evidence law for use in the later case, and prior inconsistent versions are standard fodder for cross-examination. Second, the orders: the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam, 2023, like the Evidence Act before it, treats judgments between the same parties as relevant only for limited purposes, so each court appreciates the evidence before it afresh — yet a reasoned final order is material no family judge ignores. Third, the fact of the litigation itself: respondent spouses routinely plead the filing of the DV case as an act of cruelty. Whether a complaint amounts to cruelty is a question of fact for the divorce court, and it usually turns on what became of the complaint — which is why the eventual fate of the DV case matters so much.</p>

<h2>Custody and maintenance proceedings</h2>
<p>Custody under Section 21 of the DV Act is by its own text <em>temporary</em>, granted while the application is pending. Custody proper is decided under the Guardians and Wards Act, 1890 or under Section 26 of the Hindu Marriage Act, where the welfare of the child governs by statute. The DV record enters that inquiry as material bearing on welfare: a protection order, findings about violence in the home, or equally a finding that allegations were not made out. On maintenance, the Act permits parallel claims — under Section 20 of the DV Act, Section 24 or 25 of the Hindu Marriage Act, and Section 144 of the BNSS — but Section 26(3) obliges the aggrieved person to inform the Magistrate of any relief obtained in other proceedings, and the same disclosure discipline applies in every forum. Courts adjust overlapping awards so that one income is not charged twice; the mechanics of set-off are discussed separately on this site, but the duty to disclose is the point that decides credibility.</p>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><h4>Residence and protection orders</h4><p>Surface in divorce pleadings as evidence of the state of the matrimonial home, and in custody files as material on the child’s environment.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h4>Monetary relief under Section 20</h4><p>Becomes the baseline every later maintenance forum is told about, and must be disclosed under Section 26(3) and adjusted against subsequent awards.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h4>Temporary custody under Section 21</h4><p>Creates the interim status quo that the guardianship court finds on the ground, without binding its welfare assessment.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h4>Dismissal or withdrawal</h4><p>Travels as defence material: a reasoned dismissal weakens reliance on the same incidents elsewhere, while settlement recitals can foreclose them.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Dismissal, withdrawal and their afterlife</h2>
<p>Not every DV case ends in relief, and the manner of its ending has distinct consequences. A dismissal after evidence is a reasoned adjudication: the same incidents, if re-alleged as cruelty or pressed in custody, now face a prior judicial appraisal of the very witnesses. A dismissal in default decides nothing on merits. A withdrawal, particularly one recorded as part of a settlement, equally decides nothing — but the settlement’s recitals do work of their own. Where terms record that allegations stand withdrawn or disputes resolved, a later attempt to revive the identical incidents invites the question why resolved grievances have returned. Conversely, the failure of a DV case does not automatically convert its filing into cruelty; that remains a fact-specific question the matrimonial court answers on the whole record. Strategy on both sides, therefore, lies less in the labels and more in building — or testing — the underlying evidence once, knowing it will be read many times.</p>
<div class="note"><p>Every parallel order should be placed on record in every forum, unasked. Family litigation is decided by judges who will eventually see the complete picture; the party who supplied it voluntarily is invariably better placed than the party whose suppression was discovered. How these principles apply to a given set of proceedings is fact-specific, and independent legal advice on specific facts is always advisable.</p></div>
